    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm preping a chemistry demo for my students about why cutting onions makes your eyes sting. Can you explain the specific chemical pathway from the moment the knife hits the onion to the actual irritation in the eyes? I need to explain the enzymatic part. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a knife hits an onion, the mechanical stress breaks down the cell walls of the onion cells, releasing enzymes such as Alliinase. This enzyme catalyzes the conversion of sulfur-containing compounds, like Alliin, into sulfur dioxide gas, which is then broken down into irritant gas sulfenic acid. When the sulfenic acid is in close proximity to the eyes, it reacts with water to produce sulfuric acid, an irritant that causes discomfort, pain, and stinging. This reaction is an example of an enzymatic chain reaction that leads to the release of airborne molecules.",
      "source": "target",
