    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. Could you go into a bit more detail about the specific amino acid sulfoxides involved? I want to make sure I get the nomenclature right for the worksheet. Also, is there a particular reason why the sensation feels so sudden and aggressive, almost like it's attacking the eyes specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the breakdown of Alliin through Alliinase, two main sulfoxides are produced: Allyl disulfide (also known as allyl sulfide) and methyl disulfide. These compounds are extremely volatile and can evaporate quickly, releasing their irritating effects into the air. As for the sudden and aggressive sensation, it's because of the high reactivity of these sulfoxides with the water in the eyes. When sulfenic acid reacts with the eye's water, it forms sulfuric acid, which is highly corrosive and irritant due to the high reactivity of sulfur with the eye's protective layer, the cornea, causing immediate discomfort and pain.",
      "source": "target",
